# id <TAB> upstream <TAB> meios <TAB> reason. The bidirectional reviewed divergence manifest:
# a divergence not listed here fails the differential run, a listed entry whose divergence no
# longer reproduces also fails it, and a listed entry present under the right id but with
# drifted recorded text fails it too -- matched by exact text, never by id alone. A document probe
# that refuses is recorded as REFUSED followed by the diagnostic code it gives, upstream's mapped
# through tests/refusal_causes.h. An expression probe's refusal stays REFUSED; the wording of a
# refusal is carried by expressions.cases, because a tab is this file's column separator.
#
# One measured difference is deliberately absent and is recorded here rather than as a row. A
# refused disjunction carries its left operand in its rendered text where upstream carries the
# null spelling, which is real and is pinned by a unit case. It cannot be a row: the comparison
# returns as soon as either side refuses, so a refusal's rendered text is never read, and a row
# for it would assert something this file's own mechanism never checks. The failure flag, not the
# rendered text, is what marks a refusal on this side, and that is where the contract is stated.
div_or_operand	1	True	and/or yield a boolean rather than the deciding operand (measured earlier, deliberately not changed)
div_and_operand	3	True	and/or yield a boolean rather than the deciding operand (measured earlier, deliberately not changed)
div_self_reference	[1, [1, [...]]]	REFUSED	a self-referential alias graph loads upstream as a value that contains itself; an immutable value with shared collection storage cannot denote one, so the alias arrives while its anchor is still incomplete and refuses there
div_dict_pair_sequence	{'a': 1}	REFUSED	the mapping constructor is admitted in its keyword-argument spelling alone, so a pair-sequence argument refuses here rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_dict_mixed_arguments	{'a': 1, 'b': 2}	REFUSED	a positional argument mixed with keyword arguments refuses for the same reason the pair-sequence argument does
div_string_repetition	ababab	REFUSED	a string repeated by an integer renders upstream; only the addition of two strings was measured into this grammar, so the multiplication operator refuses a string rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_string_ordering	True	REFUSED	upstream orders two strings by their code points; this grammar compares strings for equality alone, so an ordering operator against a string refuses rather than inventing a collation
div_string_split_whitespace	['a', 'b']	REFUSED	the no-argument split collapses runs of whitespace and drops the leading and trailing fields, a different algorithm from the explicit-separator form admitted here, so it refuses rather than being approximated by a single-space separator
div_string_split_limit	['a', 'b c']	REFUSED	a second argument bounds how many separators are consumed; one named operation taking one separator is admitted, so any further argument refuses
div_absolute_value	REFUSED	3	upstream's expression globals carry the mathematics module and the two reducers and no builtins at all, so the absolute value refuses there by name while this grammar answers it; it runs in the direction where this side accepts what upstream refuses, and it is the only row in that direction whose cause is the evaluator's own function set -- the two span rows that run the same way refuse upstream because the reference's span pattern truncates the span before its evaluator ever reads it. It is recorded rather than removed because the function is documented, implemented and range-checked, and dropping it would break a description this project already loads
div_span_closing_brace_in_literal	REFUSED	a}b	upstream's span pattern is quote-blind, so a closing brace inside a string literal ends the span there and the truncated fragment refuses as an unterminated literal; the close finder here folds over quotes and the whole literal renders, accepted by maintainer override on 2026-08-12, the day that fold landed
div_span_format_operator	1.234	REFUSED	the remainder operator formats a string against its right operand upstream; only arithmetic remainder was measured into this grammar, so a string operand refuses rather than taking a meaning nothing measured
div_span_nested_expression	REFUSED	3	upstream's span pattern never counts a nested brace, so an expression span written inside another ends at the inner brace and the truncated fragment refuses; the close finder here counts depth and the inner scan resolves the nested span before the outer expression is evaluated
non_text_mapping_key	<robot name="probe"><link name="from_number"></link></robot>	REFUSED expression_error	a mapping an auxiliary document keys by a number or a boolean loads on both sides, and upstream subscripts such a key by writing the key itself; every subscript key here is text, so the number reaches no entry and the document refuses rather than reading one. The refusal is raised by the subscript, not by the document parse, so this row records that the key is admitted and unreadable rather than rejected
argument_without_default	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link name="x"></link></robot>	upstream requires a default on every argument declaration and refuses one without, while here the declaration binds nothing and a caller supplies it
argument_helper_call	<robot name="probe"><link name="Aarm"></link></robot>	REFUSED unsupported_expression	upstream exposes a helper reading an argument by name inside an expression, which this grammar does not carry, so the call refuses as an unsupported call target
argument_above_declaration	REFUSED unresolved_arg	<robot name="probe"><link name="above_false"></link></robot>	the expansion here is single-forward, so a pre-pass lends, before it begins, only an argument default that lexes to itself, one holding no dollar run and no span, and a use written above its declaration resolves, while upstream reads its argument table in document order and refuses the whole document with an undefined-substitution-argument error
include_parent_directory	<robot name="probe"><link name="parent_leaf"></link></robot>	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	upstream honors a relative include filename that climbs out of the directory of the file writing it, while here a relative include climbing above its document's directory refuses as climbing above the directory of the document that writes it, a containment boundary this project keeps
include_absolute_path	<robot name="probe"><link name="absolute"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream reads an include whose filename is an absolute path, while here an absolute spelling is looked for only through the configured sources, with its leading separator removed, and refuses where none holds it
include_sole_block	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	<robot name="probe"><link name="included"></link></robot>	upstream expands a call's children first and refuses a block parameter left without an element, while here a block parameter binds what the expanded call holds and only inserting a block nobody supplied refuses
dirname_command	REFUSED unknown_substitution	<robot name="probe"><link name="dirname_command.d"></link></robot>	upstream hands its directory command no file and refuses it everywhere, while here it names the directory of the file it is written in
include_outside_link	<robot name="probe"><link name="linked_part"></link></robot>	REFUSED uncontained_asset	upstream reads a file beside the including document wherever a link there points, while here a link whose target leaves every directory that admits it (the one the including document was named in, the one it lives in, and each configured package root) refuses unless the caller follows outside links, a containment boundary this project keeps by default
include_package_filename	REFUSED unresolved_include	<robot name="probe"><link name="package_include"></link></robot>	upstream joins a package:// include filename onto the directory of the file writing it as a relative path and refuses where nothing is there, while here it is resolved through the configured sources as a package-qualified include
find_relative_root	<robot name="probe"><link name="True"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="False"></link></robot>	upstream's package lookup joins a relative configured root as written, so the directory it locates keeps the relative spelling, while here every configured root is made absolute before a lookup and the located directory is absolute; the probe compares whether the located directory equals the relative spelling, because an absolute path is not a fixed text
find_dotted_root	<robot name="probe"><link name="True"></link></robot>	<robot name="probe"><link name="False"></link></robot>	upstream's package lookup joins a configured root as written, so a current-directory element in the root stays in the directory it locates, while here a configured root's current-directory elements are removed before a lookup, resolving no link, and the located directory carries none; the probe asks whether the located directory carries that element, because the absolute root around it is not a fixed text
property_forward_reference	<robot name="probe"><link name="forward_1"></link></robot>	REFUSED undefined_property	upstream stores a property's text and evaluates it when it is read, so a property may name one written below it and a property nothing reads is never evaluated, while here a property's value is evaluated where it is written, so the forward reference refuses for the name not yet defined
include_find_climbs_out_of_package	<robot name="probe"><link name="sibling_package_part"></link></robot>	REFUSED xacro_structural_error	upstream joins a find's package directory and the rest of an include filename as written, so a parent element after the find reads a file in a sibling package, while here the rest of the filename is a path inside the find's package and leaving the package refuses, the containment every package-qualified include keeps
include_glob_filename	<robot name="probe"><link name="include_glob_filename"></link></robot>	REFUSED unresolved_include	upstream expands an include filename holding a glob character to the files it matches and includes each of them, while here include filenames are not globbed, so the pattern is looked for as a file of that name and refuses where none is
